    <p>
        Rising nearly 180 metres above the plains of the Berach and Gambhiri rivers, <strong>Chittorgarh Fort</strong> is
        the pride of Rajasthan and the largest fort in India. Spread over roughly 700 acres, it was the capital of the
        Mewar kingdom for centuries and witnessed some of the most dramatic chapters in Rajput history. Today it is a
        <strong>UNESCO World Heritage Site</strong>, inscribed in 2013 as part of the &ldquo;Hill Forts of Rajasthan&rdquo;.
    </p>
    <p>
        This guide covers the fort&rsquo;s history, the must-see monuments inside, visiting hours, ticket prices and the
        easiest way to explore it all by taxi.
    </p>

