    <h2 id="history">A Short History of Chittorgarh Fort</h2>
    <p>
        Local tradition credits the Mori (Maurya) rulers with founding the fort around the 7th century, and Bappa Rawal,
        founder of the Mewar dynasty, is said to have taken control of it in the 8th century. From then until 1568 it remained
        the seat of the Sisodia Rajputs.
    </p>
    <p>The fort is remembered above all for three great sieges:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>1303</strong> &ndash; Alauddin Khalji of Delhi besieged the fort. The story of Rani Padmini and the first <em>jauhar</em> is tied to this siege.</li>
        <li><strong>1535</strong> &ndash; Bahadur Shah of Gujarat attacked, leading to the second jauhar, led by Rani Karnavati.</li>
        <li><strong>1567&ndash;68</strong> &ndash; Mughal emperor Akbar captured the fort after the heroic defence by Jaimal and Patta. Maharana Udai Singh II then moved the capital to Udaipur.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        These stories of courage and sacrifice are why Chittorgarh is often called the soul of Rajput valour.
    </p>

    <h2 id="attractions">Top Attractions Inside Chittorgarh Fort</h2>
    <p>
        The approach road winds through seven massive gateways &ndash; Padan Pol, Bhairon Pol, Hanuman Pol, Ganesh Pol,
        Jorla Pol, Laxman Pol and finally Ram Pol &ndash; before you reach the plateau. Once inside, these are the highlights:
    </p>

    <h3>1. Vijay Stambh (Tower of Victory)</h3>
    <p>
        Built by Maharana Kumbha in the 15th century to celebrate his victory over Mahmud Khalji of Malwa, this nine-storey
        tower is about 37 metres tall and covered in intricate carvings of Hindu deities. It is the symbol of Chittorgarh.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Kirti Stambh (Tower of Fame)</h3>
    <p>
        Older than Vijay Stambh, this 12th-century Jain tower is dedicated to Adinath, the first Tirthankara. Its slender form
        and detailed sculptures make it a favourite with photographers.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Rani Padmini Palace</h3>
    <p>
        A graceful palace beside a lotus pool, with a small water pavilion in the middle. It is linked to the legend of Rani
        Padmini and is one of the most visited spots in the fort.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Rana Kumbha Palace</h3>
    <p>
        The ruins of the oldest palace in the fort, associated with Maharana Kumbha, Panna Dhai and the birth of Udai Singh II.
        The evening light and sound show is held here.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Meera Temple and Kumbha Shyam Temple</h3>
    <p>
        Two temples in the same complex: the Kumbha Shyam Temple built in the Indo-Aryan style, and the smaller temple linked
        with the poet-saint Meera Bai, the great devotee of Lord Krishna.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Kalika Mata Temple</h3>
    <p>
        Originally built as a Sun temple around the 8th century and later dedicated to Goddess Kalika, it is an active place
        of worship that draws large crowds during Navratri.
    </p>

    <h3>7. Gaumukh Kund</h3>
    <p>
        A deep, sacred reservoir fed by a spring that flows from a rock shaped like a cow&rsquo;s mouth (<em>gaumukh</em>).
        The view over the cliff edge is spectacular.
    </p>

    <h3>8. Fateh Prakash Palace Museum</h3>
    <p>
        Built by Maharana Fateh Singh, the palace now houses a museum with sculptures, weapons, paintings and artefacts from
        the region.
    </p>

